[1mRoot cause:[22m The C code uses a namespace macro [38;5;10m#define SPX_NAMESPACE(s) SPX_##s[0m which prefixes exported function names with [38;5;10mSPX_[0m. The test runner (via [38;5;10mcando2[0m) loads the shared library and looks up the symbol [38;5;10mSPX_hash_message[0m. However, the Rust translation only had an internal function named [38;5;10mhash_message[0m — it was never exported as a C-ABI symbol with the [38;5;10mSPX_[0m prefix.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mThe fix[22m (in [38;5;10mtranslated_rust/src/lib.rs[0m):[0m[0m
[0m[0m
1. Added a new exported C-ABI function:[0m[0m
  [38;5;10m[0m[38;5;10mrust
   #[unsafe(no_mangle)]
   pub unsafe extern "C" fn SPX_hash_message(
       digest: *mut u8, tree: *mut u64, leaf_idx: *mut u32,
       r: *const u8, pk: *const u8, m: *const u8,
       mlen: u64, ctx: *const SpxCtx,
   )
   [0m[38;5;10m[0m[0m[0m
  This converts raw pointers to slices and delegates to the internal implementation.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
2. Renamed the original [38;5;10mhash_message[0m to [38;5;10mhash_message_inner[0m to avoid confusion.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
3. Updated the two internal call sites ([38;5;10mcrypto_sign_signature[0m and [38;5;10mcrypto_sign_verify[0m) to call [38;5;10mhash_message_inner[0m instead.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The underlying SHA-256 implementation and [38;5;10mhash_message[0m logic were correct — the only issue was the missing symbol export with the proper namespace prefix.[0m[0m
